{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["U39", "U57", "D22", "D43"],
      "pins": ["U39.2", "U57.2"],
      "nets": ["+3V3"],
      "obs": "On the 'LED Enable and E-Stop Config' sheet, the three SN74LVC1G07 open-drain buffers used as e-stop branch indicator drivers are wired inconsistently: U56's data input (pin 2) is tied to GND, but U39's and U57's data inputs (pin 2) are tied directly to +3V3. Their /OE pins (pin 1) are pulled to +3V3 through 2.2k and driven by the CONFIG_SW DIP switch nets NET0121 / NET0145, and their open-drain outputs (pin 4) sink the LED_RED indicators D22 and D43 through 130R.",
      "expected": "For a non-inverting open-drain buffer wired as an /OE-controlled sink (the pattern the spec describes for the e-stop buffers, and the pattern actually used on U56), the data input must be held at GND so that enabling /OE pulls the output low and lights the indicator. With the input stuck at +3V3 the output is permanently high-Z, so D22 and D43 can never turn on.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "LED Enable and E-Stop Config sheet: U56 pin 2 -> GND, U39 pin 2 -> +3V3, U57 pin 2 -> +3V3; outputs pin 4 -> LED_RED D12/D22/D43 via R91/R11/R7 130R; /OE pin 1 via R68/R41/R74 2.2k to +3V3 and NET0031/NET0121/NET0145"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Changelog: 'Expand E-stop circuit to provide indicator lights for each branch'"}
      ],
      "impact": "The per-branch e-stop indicator LEDs D22 and D43 never illuminate regardless of the CONFIG_SW setting, so two of the three branch status lights added by the changelog are non-functional; the board's e-stop indication behaves differently from the intended design.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The CAN crystals are correctly 16 MHz and the major interfaces match the spec, but on the e-stop indicator section two of the three open-drain buffers (U39, U57) have their data input tied to +3V3 instead of GND (unlike U56), so their LED_RED indicators can never sink current and the per-branch status lights are dead."
}

